[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says every report in it is one bug: the parser reads dates month first.
Read report by report, it holds three problems. Each has its own ticket now, and each needs its own fix.

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix queued for #4471 fixes <code>parser</code> only. The other two keep coming in after it ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Counted from the reports export on every build: the numbers move as the desk merges more.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** A tenant that writes the day first
books 7 April and gets 4 July. It reaches every channel, at every tenant on file.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** People type month first, as the hint
tells them, and the form reads what they typed the tenant's way, day first. Only reports through the
form, only at tenants on booking-v3. Fixing the parser does not touch it: the hint is wrong, not the reading.

**`shift`: the date slips by one day.** Day and month stay put: Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May.
Every booking in June one day off; the same meeting on Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto; an
invite that arrives before the booking. That is a time-zone or daylight-saving conversion, not a date
order. The desk listed these reports in its notes, then filed them under `parser` by tenant.

## How they ended up in one ticket

- **Subject lines.** [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone.
  "Wrong day on the invite" fits all three problems, so all three were merged as one.
- **Intake's tag.** `intakeTag` is a guess from the reporter's browser. Everyone on an en-US browser got
  `hint`, whatever their tenant runs. The cause depends on the tenant's locale and form (`locales.ts`).
- **The old table.** The #4471 cause table marks the biggest group as the ticket and the rest
  "no ticket of its own", and footnotes the one-day moves inside `parser`.
- **Overlapping export pages.** The same report comes twice; count each id once.

## When you triage the next one

1. Count unique ids, not rows.
2. Place each report by tenant and channel (`causes.ts`), not by subject or intake tag.
3. A report that is neither a flipped date nor a booking-v3 form entry is its own problem until someone shows otherwise.

